Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.510 align:start position:0%
 
can we start with what you may have  heard i know you couldn't see the  pictures but you heard from  your people on the streets of the  capital who want your government to be  tougher  what would you say to them i think  the message we've already sent is that  we will use  every diplomatic and political option to  pursue  a path at the united nations as well to  show the world  what india has done let's consider what  india has done by this action which is  illegal  illegitimate and unlawful what they have  done  is to fly in the face of security  council resolutions that have been there  on the council's agenda for 70 years 11  security council resolution they have  violated them  they have violated international law  they don't wish to talk to pakistan  to find a peaceful settlement of this  outstanding dispute  and as your new story showed they have  turned  14 million people over 14 million people  in occupied kashmir into prisoners this  is an armed camp  that they have created and this is a  serious situation  which poses a grave threat to peace and  security  in our region i wonder i do i do want to  ask sorry to intro i do want to ask you  about your talks at the u.n  but first just what you were talking  about in reaction to what the people in  islamabad were saying  is it fair to say there's some mixed  messaging coming from pakistan  your prime minister said five day uh  said uh very recently in fact that he  doesn't want war it would be absolutely  disastrous  is what he said in parliament but your  chief of the army  was saying the pakistan army stands  firmly by the kashmiris we are prepared  and will go to any extent  to to fulfill our obligations that  sounds like war is on the table  no war is not on the table pakistan does  not wish to see a conflict pakistan will  not do anything to escalate the  situation  the aggressor and the country that's  escalated the situation and take us  taken us to the edge of the precipice is  india and i think what we will do at the  united nations  is to take up the issue with the  security council after all  the un has outstanding responsibilities  to deal with this issue  there are security council resolutions  as i explained to you earlier  and what we had yesterday was a very  strong statement from the secretary  general of the united nations  which validated these council  resolutions and said  this issue must be resolved according to  the u.n charter  and the u.n charter what is integral in  the u.n charter  is the right of self-determination i  think what's at stake here  is the destiny the future and the lives  of  over 14 million people who've been  turned into hostages  there is huge military presence in  occupied jammu and kashmir  so i think what you will see my country  doing is to take this issue  up at the diplomatic level and use all  political means to ensure  that we get justice for the kashmiri  people i think our message  to the kashmiri people is you're not  alone you will never be alone  we are with you and we will continue to  do whatever we can  to ensure that your plight look tom  ultimately this issue  in the first instance this issue is  about human suffering  it's about the plight of a people who've  been deprived of their liberty for over  70 years  and now they're on the edge of being  deprived of everything else  they cannot leave their homes their  leaders are in have been arrested  they their food shortages and above all  now there is an assault on their very  identity  so pakistan will stand with the people  of kashmir and we will do whatever we  can  to ensure that there is justice you  rightly call out those human rights  abuses and you've just said you know we  are with you the people of kashmir  you highlight their human suffering  correctly but is it slightly  hypocritical given that pakistan was on  a list of countries who praised china's  contribution to international human  rights causes  for what large proportions of the  international community consider  internment in forced labour camps in  xinjiang who are  all of whom are muslims how why are we  changing the narrative why are we  changing the subject well you're called  you're rightly calling you're rightly  calling out human rights abuses  you're rightly human rights abuses the  issue  you should do across the board the issue  is of  international law there are security  council resolutions as i said  there are two reports of the united  nations on human rights violations in  occupied kashmir  we have to keep the focus on a crisis  that is brewing  in our region we don't want this crisis  to spin out of control  we would like the international  community to step up to the plate  to stand on the side of law justice  and fairness and also to deliver on the  pledges that were made to the people of  kashmir by the indian leadership itself  years and years ago indian leaders  pledged and promised a plebiscite  for the people of jammu and kashmir so  that they could freely  decide their destiny so if you believe  in democracy  and if you believe in liberty and if you  believe in human rights  why is it denied to the people of  occupied kashmir i think but you don't  think but you don't think that  those same rights you should stand up  for them for people in other countries  very close to you  in china that's not the issue that's not  the issue under discussion  right now uh what we will be taking up  with the security council  is the issue which is before us before  this crisis  spins out of control okay let me ask you  about that about antonio gutierrez and  the un in general then do you do you  really have confidence  in the un stepping up to the plate  because his statement which was  supportive  of you also said that he recalls the  1972 agreement on bilateral relations  between india and pakistan  which states that the final status of  jammu and kashmir is to be settled by  peaceful means do you think he's  distancing the u.n from a key role in  this with that statement not at all  not at all by validating and referring  to u.n security council resolutions  by also expressing his concern that  these restrictions are going to  exacerbate the human rights situation in  occupied kashmir  we are satisfied with this statement and  we really think  that it will be up to the security  council now to address this issue  it is a major issue it is a major threat  to international peace and security  and also it is an issue of whether we  want to live  in a rules-based international order  where people abide by security council  resolutions  or do we want to live in a world where  countries get up  and you know unilaterally take actions  that pose a threat  to not just their neighbor but to the  whole world actually  can i ask you about the reaction of  muslim majority countries saudi arabia  was part of a statement that was  released but they've refrained from  taking a position there are reports that  your prime minister personally  telephoned the leaders of some muslim  majority states  and there's been a muted reaction indeed  the united arab emirates arab emirates  has praised  india arguing that the revocation of  article 370 will improve social justice  and security are you disappointed  in the reaction of some of your what tom  tom for a start i think you've had a way  somebody's given you very selected  uh press uh reports so the prime  minister hasn't spoken  the prime minister hasn't spoken to any  muslim majority i have just had uh  i've just had a meeting with the saudi  ambassador to the united nations he's  he's expressed his full support for my  country's position here at the u.n  i have been meeting with other muslim  countries here we have had a strong  statement  from the secretary general of the oic  which is the organization of islamic  countries  we are confident that our muslim  brothers and sisters will stand by us  but the question i have to ask  western countries who lecture the whole  world about human rights  what about the human rights of the  people of occupied kashmir  i mean you take up kajals for everyone  else would you like therefore to hear  what would you like  to hear more from the united states and  the united kingdom we we would certainly  like to hear  more from the united kingdom which is  responsible  uh for the dispute in the first place uh  let's not forget  this is something which isn't the  unfinished business  of a colonial era so yes we certainly  would like to hear but you know above  all tom  what we'd like countries to take  positions on is the issue  of the plight of a people who were made  promises  these promises are enshrined in u.n  security council resolutions  those were agreed these are  international documents  they were signed countries signed up to  them so the question we have to ask is  are these security council resolutions  going to be cast aside  and are we going to allow geopolitics to  color  our position on this and i think  ultimately countries will come down on  the side  of justice and of course on the side of  peace because above all i mean the mood  that i find at the u.n  and i can share that with you is one of  shock  and deep concern about the action that  the indian government has taken  my country has not though you don't  sense there's a reluctance to want to  get involved at the u.n  i think countries are being lobbied and  briefed right now  uh all of these diplomacy is a process  it's not an event  and it doesn't take place as a news  story so it takes a while  to get people to be fully briefed so  that know the full picture  plus look at the situation uh on the  ground in occupied german kashmir  what your reporter just you know sent  you a news report  what does that say what that says is  that the curfew  and the restrictions on people's very  movement  is in place what will happen after all  curfews  are not permanent what will happen when  these curfews  come off when they end the anger  of the kashmiri people will be heard  loud and clear  across the world just one fight sorry  we're running slightly we're running  slightly short on time i do just want to  ask you one one final question apologies  for interrupting and um when it comes to  the president of the united states  imran khan seems to have asked him for  some help previously  was that the right decision yes of  course it's the right decision  you know you mentioned the similar  agreement in a bilateral dialogue  when the other country does not want to  be a partner for peace  we then look towards the world and we  look towards mediators we look towards  the united nations  uh we are ready for bilateral  multilateral any format  so long as we can get justice for the  people of occupied jammu and kashmir

